---
import { loadFeed } from '../lib/feed.ts';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'; import { COMMON } from '../lib/methodology.ts'; import { RATES, CHARGES_VERSION } from '../lib/charges.ts';  import { ledger } from '../lib/data.ts'; import { dmy } from '../lib/view.ts'; import { GLOSSARY, KINDS } from '../lib/glossary.ts';
const L = ledger(); const F = loadFeed(); const weights = F.meta.weights ?? {};
---
<Base title="Method" description="How every figure on Rulebook is made: the ledger, the accounting, how calls are chosen, the data sources, every number defined once, and what changed and why.">
<div class="wrap hero prose"><span class="label eyebrow">Method</span><h1>How every figure is made.</h1></div>
<div class="wrap prose">
<h2 data-u="mt6">The method, in short</h2>
<details class="table-alt" open><summary>One ledger, append-only</summary><p>A single record, rebuilt each trading night, holds the screens, every weekly run, every call, trade and void, and one book snapshot a day. New nights add rows and never rewrite old ones; a test fails the build if they try. Pages format it and never compute their own version. As of {dmy(L.as_of)}.</p></details>
<details class="table-alt"><summary>The accounting, with a worked example</summary><ul><li><strong>Realised</strong> is what exits have booked, partial exits included, after their share of charges.</li><li><strong>Unrealised</strong> is the remaining quantity against its remaining cost, after the buy charges that belong to it.</li><li><strong>Every charge is counted once</strong>, attached to one fill.</li><li><strong>R</strong> is measured against the original risk to that call's own stop, set at entry and unchanged by partial exits.</li></ul><p>Before charges: buy 100 at ₹100 with a stop at ₹90, sell 50 at ₹120, mark the other 50 at ₹110. Realised ₹1,000, unrealised ₹500, total ₹1,500, initial risk ₹1,000, so 1.5R. The site's tests run this example every time.</p></details>
<details class="table-alt"><summary>How calls are chosen</summary><p>{COMMON.book}</p><p>{COMMON.mechanics}</p><p>{COMMON.size}</p></details>
<details class="table-alt"><summary>What the simulation leaves out</summary><p>{COMMON.basis}</p><p>Real trades have slippage, partial fills, taxes on gains and halts that this simulation ignores or simplifies, which makes the simulated record more flattering than a real one would be.</p><p>{COMMON.lag}</p><p>{COMMON.missing}</p></details>
<details class="table-alt"><summary>Charges and benchmark</summary><p>Schedule {CHARGES_VERSION}: delivery equity. Securities transaction tax {RATES.sttPct}% on both sides, exchange transaction charge {RATES.exchangePct}%, SEBI fee {RATES.sebiPct}%, stamp duty {RATES.stampBuyPct}% on buys, GST {RATES.gstPct}% on exchange and SEBI fees, and a flat ₹{RATES.dpSellFlat} depository charge on sells. Rates are starting values, to be checked against the official schedules. The benchmark is the Nifty 500 price index, which excludes dividends; the simulated calls exclude them too, so the two are compared on the same basis.</p></details>
<details class="table-alt"><summary>Sample size</summary><p>Every statistic shows the number of closed trades behind it. Under 30, it carries the label "too few to conclude anything". The record is new and takes months to reach that size.</p></details>
<h2 data-u="mt6" id="price-basis">How to read the numbers</h2>
<p>Daily-feed figures and the public record use dated closing prices. Delayed quotes can come from a later session: their timestamp is the exchange print time, not the time you opened this page. The Last price and P&amp;L in a call row use the same quote. A missing quote leaves that row at its labelled ledger close.</p>
<p>P&amp;L before charges is quantity × (last price − entry price). After charges also deducts the costs attached to the fills. Cash is the starting capital plus sell proceeds, minus buy costs and all charges. The record's return is cumulative since inception, not a daily return; drawdown includes entry charges from the initial zero-return baseline. If the benchmark's starting close is missing, its return stays missing.</p>
<p>Market pulse, regime and barometer are separate dated sources. Values from different sessions are not directly comparable. Peer counts include only companies with the particular metric, so their denominators can differ.</p>
<h2 data-u="mt6">Data</h2><p>{COMMON.sources}</p>
<h2 data-u="mt6" id="numbers">Every number, defined once</h2><p>Each figure on the site is one of three kinds. <strong>Fact</strong> is {KINDS.Fact}. <strong>Model</strong> is {KINDS.Model}. <strong>Result</strong> is {KINDS.Result}. Rulebook publishes no opinions. If a figure on a page disagrees with this list, this list is the one to trust, and please tell us.</p>
<p class="src">Composite weights from the feed dated {dmy(F.asOf)}: {Object.entries(weights).map(([k, v]) => `${k} ${typeof v === "number" ? Math.round(v * 100) + "%" : "missing"}`).join(" · ") || "not supplied"}. We reproduce the feed’s score; these weights alone do not verify the inputs or every component’s calculation.</p><div class="gloss">{GLOSSARY.map((g) => <details class="table-alt"><summary><span class="kind" data-kind={g.kind}>{g.kind}</span> {g.term}</summary><p>{g.what}</p><p><strong>How it is computed.</strong> {g.how}</p><p><strong>Wrong if.</strong> {g.wrongIf}</p></details>)}</div>
<h2 data-u="mt6" id="changes">Published method notes</h2><p>The current paper book uses version 2 screens, first run on 17 November 2026. Each screen lists up to five names; a listed stock is not necessarily taken. The Calls page carries each run's date and exit rules. Internal implementation decisions are kept in the repository.</p>
</div></Base>
